WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.979
Film Courage: Fatih, I would love to hear
your process for making the playlist using

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:07.129
Queens of the Stone Age for [IN THE FADE’s
protagonist] Katja and how you sort of developed

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:09.760
her emotions within their songs?

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:17.039
Fatih Akin: Finding the right music in the
early stage is always very important for me

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:22.380
that I have an access to my own material and
emotional access because music is all about

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.060
emotions.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.849
And I was looking for what is the right tone
for this?

00:00:27.849 --> 00:00:33.920
And the tone it has to be something which
is melancholic and aggressive at the same

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.000
time.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.200
And I was like “Okay, that’s Queen’s
music!”

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.690
So I made up this list of certain songs of
all the Queens of the Stone Age albums and

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:48.070
I gave the playlist to Diane.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:54.510
It was an instinctive thought you know of
songs and Diane immediately responded to it,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:55.510
very positively.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.930
She wasn’t like “I hate the music.”

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.760
You know, she was not like that at all.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.710
She said it felt right.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:08.280
So I get in touch with Josh Homme [frontman
of Queens of the Stone Age].

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.990
And I told him “Hey, I’m this filmmaker
from Germany and I’m doing this film.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:14.820
And it’s a bit tiny and it’s about this
and that.”

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.930
And he said “Can you send me the film?”

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.790
So I sent him the very rough cut.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:26.510
He immediately liked the film a lot and he
asked me “Does the score have to be rock?”

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:30.180
And I said “Josh, do whatever you feel is
right.”

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.130
And he did it and I think he made the perfect
soundtrack for it.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.490
Film Courage: Absolutely.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.510
[Diane] On a film set, is a director always
right?

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.570
Diane Kruger: Uh, 98 percent, yes.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.230
Film Courage: What about the other 2 percent?

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:52.159
Diane Kruger: The other 2 percent I feel like
sometimes an actor has an instinct and that

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:58.410
goes against the instinct of the director
and when you propose it, it turns out sometimes

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.760
the actor is right, you know?

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.660
But I would always, always first go with the
director’s instinct and then you can just

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.280
sort of fine tune it, maybe?

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:15.740
Film Courage: Fatih, do you have any instances
where there was a scene and Diane suggested

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.810
one other way and how it worked?

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:23.340
I mean are you sort of married to one idea
or are you very fluid in terms of how you

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.330
approach a filming day?

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.410
Fatih Akin: I work fluidly.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:34.280
I work very close with my actors and I don’t
know everything, you know?

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:41.270
It’s like…I don’t have the impression
that it’s 98 percent.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:43.069
I think it’s less, you know?

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:49.250
I don’t mean it as a humble thing, I mean
it as it is.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:54.410
For me working on the set, I like every aspect
of it.

00:02:54.410 --> 00:03:01.330
I like the technical aspect, I like the camera,
I like the writing, I like the breaks to do

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:02.330
the food.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:05.650
But what I like the most on set to work on
is with the actors.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:07.860
That’s how everything starts for me.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.730
That’s the thing that interests me the most.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:18.340
And here I really had an exchange that I came
up with half an idea and Diane has half an

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.870
idea and together we make one idea out of
it, which is right.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:29.000
In the very last shot of the trial of the
film where there is this [camera] trekking

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:39.590
back and zooming in scene, I had the idea
that Diane has to act differently than what

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.590
she is doing in the film.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:45.090
Because the technical element of that shot
was so difficult.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:50.150
I mean 7 or 8 takes and maybe Diane was tired
of that emotion?

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:51.800
I don’t know, I don’t remember?

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.700
But all she felt like was that it doesn’t
feel right.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:59.849
She said that after the 12th or 13th takes,
she said “Can I suggest something?

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:01.370
I would like to do it differently.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:02.910
I would like to do nothing.”

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:04.790
And I was like “Yeah, go ahead.”

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:12.870
And she made it and first of all, that was
the best take in terms of technically.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.510
And you know it was like a spiritual thing.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:21.919
It was like that was right because she gave
the right emotion, the camera filmed it in

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:22.919
the right way.

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:25.960
I believe in these things, you know?

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:32.460
I saw her attitude, her idea and I used it
for the whole last part of the film (what

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.099
she’d given in there).

00:04:34.099 --> 00:04:37.800
So it was more than 98 percent, I tell you.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.110
I mean less, sorry!

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:44.110
It was less than 98 percent.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:49.380
Film Courage: Even under ideal circumstances
everyone has days where even if they love

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.689
a project, it gets to be a lot.

00:04:51.689 --> 00:04:54.090
You know, different things happen, life happens.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:59.110
So what keeps you going with a project even
when it seems like the Universe is sort of

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:05.220
conspiring against you, which tends to happen
even with something that you love?

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:06.939
What keeps you going with a project?

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:11.699
Even when it seems like there are all of these
things in your way and you stay with it?

00:05:11.699 --> 00:05:16.449
Fatih Akin: The tiny little voice in your
ear.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:21.180
It really is there and everybody can say “I
don’t like the screenplay and I don’t

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.430
think it’s bankable.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:26.719
I don’t think anybody will see it.”

00:05:26.719 --> 00:05:30.599
I hear these things, especially from this
film, based on that screenplay.

00:05:30.599 --> 00:05:34.540
I heard that a lot from very close people
around me.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:42.409
But there’s an inner voice when those people
cannot touch this voice, then you know it’s

00:05:42.409 --> 00:05:44.150
right and then you go on.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:49.270
Sometimes if they can touch this voice, they
may be right and you start to question it.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:55.509
Diane Kruger: Well, once you are committed
to a project, you’re sort of in it.

00:05:55.509 --> 00:05:57.800
There’s no way of having a bad day.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:06.069
I think at least the times when I’ve been
on a film set and I felt like the movie is

00:06:06.069 --> 00:06:13.159
not going to be great or the director is not
all that great or I don’t know…whatever

00:06:13.159 --> 00:06:18.789
you can feel in that moment, it’s your duty
to try and elevate it as much as you can.

00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:24.809
There’s never been a moment in my life on
the set where I felt like giving up.

00:06:24.809 --> 00:06:27.319
You always try and it doesn’t always work
out.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:30.300
We all know that some movies come out better
than others.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.289
But you have to try, like sometimes even harder
because of it.

